.TH CFAB 8 "September 2026" "cfab 0.5.3" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
cfab \- per-host runtime of the resilient converged fabric for small clusters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cfab
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIMEMBER\fR]
.I subcommand
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cfab
applies, verifies, and removes the converged network fabric on one cluster
member. The fabric is declared once, in
.BR fabric.toml ;
everything else \(em segment sub-interfaces, the nft forward policy and
traffic-class marking, per-NIC HTB shaping trees, and the FRR
(OSPF/BFD/BGP) configuration \(em is derived from that declaration and
generated by this binary. Physical NICs are transport; traffic classes
(storage, cluster, management) are policy layered above them: connectivity is
additive, failover automatic, and control traffic protected under saturation.
.PP
Both member kinds need
.BR ip (8).
A
.B host
needs
.BR nft (8):
it installs
.BR "table inet cfab" ,
which carries the per-zone bulk DSCP clamp and the fallback control-egress
ceiling, and a kernel without nf_tables is a hard refusal for that kind. A
.B leaf
takes
.BR nft (8)
too where the kernel has it, and otherwise falls back to
.BR iptables-legacy (8),
.BR iptables-legacy-save (8)
and
.BR iptables-legacy-restore (8),
installing the ceiling ALONE \(em that kernel has no DSCP target, so the
per-zone bulk clamp is skipped and
.B cfab status
names the backend and says so. The choice is made at
.B up
on the kernel's own refusal and recorded in the run dir. This member's own
OSPF and BFD are marked by the engine itself (DSCP CS6, and skb-priority
PCP_CTRL onto the wire through the segment sub-interface's egress-qos-map),
not by that table, which lets them past the clamp untouched. A
.B host
additionally needs
.BR tc (8)
for its shaping trees; a
.B leaf
shapes nothing \(em its wires' qdiscs belong to its own OS. A missing
.BR nft (8)
or
.BR tc (8)
is refused by name before anything is applied.
.PP
.BR ethtool (8)
is used by both kinds if it is installed, to record each present wire's
driver and cross-check its link speed \(em cfab sets no NIC feature any
more (that is a host\-side udev rule now, fired on the netdev\-add event).
It is a read\-only diagnostic dependency (Debian
.BR Recommends ,
not
.BR Depends ):
absent, \fBup\fR still applies with an empty driver record and one
warning, and
.B status
reports
.B "driver ?"
and names the gap once instead of refusing anything. If ethtool is present
but one device refuses
.BR "ethtool \-i" ,
.B up
warns
.B "WARNING: ethtool \-i <wire>: driver unrecorded"
for that wire alone; it gets no driver record, and the forwarding watchdog
skips its different\-driver check for it.
.PP
Most subcommands require root. The generators
.RB ( "cfab gen" ,
.BR "cfab check" ,
.BR "cfab schema" )
are pure: they read the declaration and change nothing.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Parse and validate
.BR fabric.toml ;
print the resolved view for this member (zones, segments, wires, ingress
legs). Validation fails loudly on any inconsistency; an unknown key in the
declaration is a parse error naming its line and column.
.TP
.B schema
Print the
.B fabric.toml
declaration schema (the file an operator writes, not cfab's internal model) as
JSON Schema. Needs no config file.
.TP
.BR "gen policy" | "mark [--backend nft|iptables-legacy]" | engine
Print a generated artifact: the nft forward policy (table inet cfab-fwd:
default-deny for anything touching a cfab interface, declared pairs pass, the
admin NIC never transits, packets touching no cfab interface are accepted as
another stack's business), the traffic-class marking (table inet cfab: the
per-zone bulk DSCP clamp plus one derived control-egress ceiling per fallback
bond \(em or, with
.BR --backend " iptables-legacy" ,
the ceiling alone as an
.BR iptables-legacy-restore (8)
input, which is what a leaf installs on a kernel without nf_tables), or
this member's routing-engine configuration tree (JSON).
.TP
.BI "gen shape " DEV " " [\fB\-\-tc\fR|\fB\-\-expect\fR]
Print the floor+borrow HTB derivation for one physical NIC.
.B \-\-tc
prints the tc program instead;
.B \-\-expect
prints the "classid effective-rate" lines
.B status
diffs against the live qdisc tree.
.TP
.B run
Apply the fabric on this member \(em segment sub-interfaces, policy, marking,
shaping, and routing-engine config \(em then stay in the foreground and
supervise the engine, the shape daemon, and (only when pmxcfs reports a
cluster) conf-sync as direct child processes. This is the long-lived
.B "systemd Type=notify"
service and the container PID 1; it is not run by hand. The apply is
idempotent.
.B SIGHUP
re\-reads the declaration and acts on it (see
.B RELOAD
below), while
.B SIGTERM
runs the stop sequence: forwarding off first, then each child is signalled and
given 10 s before SIGKILL, then the fabric is torn down. Only one supervisor
runs at a time, guarded by a lock on the run dir.
.TP
.B down
Remove everything cfab applied and restore FRR to its pre-fabric state. Never
touches interfaces or firewall state the fabric does not own. Refuses while a
supervisor is still running.
.TP
.BR status " [\fB\-\-wait\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-permissive\fR]"
What this member's fabric is doing right now, in one of four states:
.BR UP ", " UP\-DEGRADED ", " FAILED " or " DOWN
(exit 0, 1, 2, 3). The headline carries three fixed counts,
.RI ( peers " | " links " | " fallbacks ),
each
.IR n / N :
.B peers
= peers with at least one available adjacency (self is never counted);
.B links
= BFD sessions, one per (peer, zone, segment) the two members share;
.B fallbacks
= OSPF neighbors on the fallback segment, one per peer carrying that zone's
row, available at 2\-Way or better. All three denominators come from
.BR fabric.toml ,
so a cable pull moves the numerator and never the denominator. A member
declaring no fallback row prints
.BR 0/0 ,
never a missing field.
.IP
One indented line per condition follows the headline, sorted, each condition
named once.
.B status
never writes: a condition that makes a link unsafe is brought down by
.BR "cfab fwd\-watchdog" ,
and the state then follows from the counts; everything else is a reason line
that does not move the state.
.IP
.B status
describes the fabric that is
.IR running ,
not the file: the supervisor keeps the declaration it applied at
.IR RUN_DIR/fabric.toml.applied ,
and
.B status
prefers that copy, so an operator editing
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
\(em or one whose bad edit
.B reload
has just refused \(em still gets the state of the fabric instead of a parse
error. Every other subcommand keeps reading the file: they act on what is
declared. When the file on disk disagrees with the running fabric,
.B status
adds one reason line and the state is unchanged. An unreadable, unparseable or
invalid file \(em including one that no longer declares this member \(em reads
.IP
.nf
declaration /etc/cfab/fabric.toml: \fIerror\fP (status describes the running
fabric; a reload of this file will be refused)
.fi
.IP
with the parser's own line, column and snippet, its continuation lines
indented; a valid file that differs from what is running reads
.IP
.nf
declaration /etc/cfab/fabric.toml changed since apply (systemctl reload cfab
to apply; the fabric will restart)
.fi
.IP
Equality is semantic, so a comment or whitespace edit is not a change. The
copy lives with the fabric:
.B down
removes the run dir whole and takes it along.
.IP
.B \-\-wait
.I N
re\-reads every 2\ s until the state is UP or
.I N
seconds pass, then reports whatever state was reached \(em there is no
"not converged" verdict.
.B DOWN
ends the wait at once (there is no intent to wait for);
.B FAILED
and
.B UP\-DEGRADED
do not, because a fabric coming up passes through both. Default
.BR "\-\-wait 0" ,
one instant read.
.B \-\-permissive
exits 0 for
.B UP\-DEGRADED
as well as
.BR UP ;
it never masks
.B FAILED
or
.BR DOWN .
.IP
.B Bootstrap:
the first member of a fresh fabric has no peer to be adjacent to, so it
honestly reads
.BR "FAILED (0/P | 0/L | 0/F)" ,
and
.B \-\-permissive
does not hide it. The second member turns both UP.
.TP
.BI "measure\-cap " "DEV PEER " [ SECS ]
Flood
.I PEER
on
.I DEV
for
.I SECS
seconds (default 6) with an unmarked native UDP flood and record the wire's
measured capacity to the cap file the shape derivation reads. On a clustered
member the flood runs under the
.I priv/lock/cfab\-measure
cluster lease (one measurement at a time cluster-wide; if another member holds
it, the run refuses and says so \(em retry shortly), and the measured cap is
also published to
.IR /etc/pve/cfab/caps/MEMBER/cap\-DEV ,
where the shape derivation finds it again should the local (tmpfs) cap file be
lost; a publication failure is a warning, never a measurement failure. Refuses
to run while the fabric's shaper holds
.IR DEV 's
egress qdisc \(em the shape-daemon child of
.B cfab run
holds it while the fabric is up, and the measurement needs the qdisc free.
.TP
.B policy\-teeth
Prove the forward policy in throwaway network namespaces \(em every zone pair
exercised against the declared allow-list \(em and then prove the proof
bites, by regressing the policy in memory and requiring the matrix to go red.
Cleans up its namespaces even on failure.
.TP
.B shape\-daemon
Membership-reactive shaping loop; a supervised child of
.B cfab run
on host members. Not intended for interactive use.
.TP
.B fwd\-watchdog
One fail-closed forwarding-posture check. Inside
.B cfab run
this runs as an in-process task on a few-second tick (it replaces a systemd
timer); the subcommand runs a single check by hand.
If the forward policy is not loaded with a drop default, it disables
forwarding on every cfab interface and exits nonzero. A cfab interface whose
forwarding flag drifted from the declared value (a foreign stack writing
.I ip_forward=1
propagates onto every interface) is written back and logged. Interfaces cfab
does not own are never read or written: another forwarder on the host (Docker,
a routed bridge, a host-level CNI) is not cfab's to police.
.TP
.B cluster status
Report the pmxcfs coordination state: presence, quorum (the
.I /etc/pve/local
owner-write-bit probe), the member list with online flags, the published
.B fabric.toml
(generation, size, first line), and the published measured caps (one line per
member). When
.I /etc/pve
is absent or pmxcfs runs in local mode, prints a clean \(lqnot clustered\(rq
report and exits 0 \(em coordination is additive, never a dependency.
.TP
.B conf publish
Validate the local
.B fabric.toml
fully (a declaration that does not validate is refused, never published),
require a quorate cluster, then publish it cluster-wide: take the
.I priv/lock/cfab\-conf
cluster lock (with stale-lock recovery after the 120\ s pmxcfs expiry), write
.I /etc/pve/cfab/fabric.toml
atomically (temp file + rename), bump
.IR /etc/pve/cfab/gen ,
and release the lock. Ack directories older than the previous generation are
then removed (best effort).
.TP
.B conf\-sync
Cluster config-sync daemon; a supervised child of
.B cfab run
on every clustered member (publisher included \(em it is not special). Not
intended for interactive use. Every second it checks quorum (non-quorate:
do nothing, reads may be stale) and the published generation. On a new
generation it validates the published conf with the full gate (an invalid
publish is refused loudly and never applied, and that generation is never
retried), caches the current
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
to
.IR fabric.toml.prev ,
installs the published text, asks the supervisor to re-apply it over
.BR cfab.sock ,
and runs
.B status
(exit 0 or 1 \(em UP or UP\-DEGRADED \(em counts as carrying traffic). It then
writes its ack file and
waits up to 60\ s for an ack from at least one
.I different
member. No peer ack, a
.B status
of FAILED or DOWN, or a failed ack write (the severed\-node
signal: a member that cannot write acks cannot see them either) \(em all
REVERT to the previous conf and re-apply it.
A byte-identical publish acks and commits without applying. A single-member
cluster skips the witness wait.
.IP
.B Note (2-node clusters):
a lone applier whose peer is down reverts after the 60\ s window \(em by
design, the fail-safe refuses an unwitnessed conf; re-publish once the peer
is back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " PATH"
Path to
.BR fabric.toml .
Default search order: a
.B fabric.toml
beside the binary, then
.IR /etc/cfab/fabric.toml ,
then
.B ./fabric.toml
in the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " MEMBER"
The `[[member]]` row to run as. Default:
.BR $CFAB_HOST ,
else the kernel hostname.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Standard help and version output.
.SH RELOAD
.B "systemctl reload cfab"
(SIGHUP, or a
.B reapply
request on the supervisor socket \(em one code path) re\-reads
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
and compares the fabric it derives with the running one. Comments and whitespace do
not count: the comparison is of the derived fabric, not the file bytes. Reloads never
overlap: one arriving during another queues behind it.
.TP
.B unchanged
Re\-applied in place: no teardown, no netdev churn, but the engine and the shape
daemon restart \(em measured 2026\-09\-06 on the testbed, 6\(en10 s of loss on every
zone while OSPF re\-converges.
.TP
.B "changed and valid"
The fabric is torn down and the unit restarted onto the new declaration (exit 6). An
in\-place apply cannot reach it: the apply creates and repairs, it never removes what
the previous declaration had. The whole fabric is down for the restart, as on a
package upgrade.
.TP
.B "invalid, unreadable, or no longer declaring this host"
Refused. The running fabric is kept exactly as it was, and
.B "cfab status"
names the refusal on its
.B components:
line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B status
maps its state to the exit code, Nagios\-style (0/1/2/3).
.B run
uses 0/1/3/4/6: 0 on a clean stop, 1 on a runtime failure, 3 when the apply is
refused, 4 when another supervisor already holds the lock, 6 when a reload found a
changed declaration. 3 and 4 are the unit's
.BR RestartPreventExitStatus ,
neither fixed by trying again; 6 is its
.B SuccessExitStatus
and
.BR RestartForceExitStatus .
Every other subcommand uses 0 for success and 1 for failure.
.TP
.B 0
.B status
.BR UP :
every expected adjacency available. For
.BR run ,
a clean stop \(em the SIGTERM teardown completed. For every other subcommand,
success.
.TP
.B 1
.B status
.BR UP\-DEGRADED :
up and carrying traffic, but some expected adjacency is down (a wire pulled, a
session not yet established) \(em 0 under
.BR \-\-permissive .
For
.BR run ,
a runtime failure that systemd retries after
.BR RestartSec .
For every other subcommand, failure: a validation error, a failed apply, a
bitten watchdog, or
.B policy\-teeth
finding the policy (or its own teeth) broken. A
.B fabric.toml
that is present but unparseable also exits 1, from any subcommand
.B status
included \(em we cannot know what was desired.
.TP
.B 2
.B status
.BR FAILED :
no adjacency is available at all while up is desired.
.B \-\-permissive
does not mask it.
.B run
does not use this code.
.TP
.B 3
.B status
.BR DOWN :
up is not desired: there is no run dir \(em
.B cfab down
was run, or
.B cfab run
never was \(em or there is no
.B fabric.toml
on this host at all.
.B \-\-permissive
does not mask it. For
.BR run ,
the declaration or this host refused the apply; retrying will not help.
.TP
.B 4
.B run
only: another supervisor already holds the lock on the run dir. Retrying will
not help.
.TP
.B 6
.B run
only: a reload found a changed declaration. The stop sequence completed \(em this is
a clean stop, not a failure \(em and the unit is restarted to apply the new file. Note
that 5 belongs to a supervised child that could not arm its parent\-death signal, not
to this.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CFAB_HOST
Member name to run as when
.B \-\-host
is not given.
.TP
.B CFAB_UP_IFS
Space-separated authoritative up-set for
.BR "gen shape" ;
overrides sysfs carrier detection.
.TP
.B CFAB_CAP_DIR
Directory holding
.BI cap\- DEV
measured-capacity files (default: the declaration's run directory).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
Site declaration (not shipped; an example is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/cfab/examples/ ).
On a clustered member,
.B conf\-sync
maintains it as the last-known-good cluster conf.
.TP
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml.prev
The
.B conf\-sync
revert target: the conf that was live before the last cluster apply.
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/fabric.toml.applied
The declaration the supervisor applied, written before its initial apply and
removed with the run dir by
.BR down .
.B status
reads it so it can describe the running fabric while
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
is mid\-edit or refused. Found through the
.B run_dir
the file declares, else the packaged default; a member that both moved
.B run_dir
and has an unparseable file leaves nothing on disk to say where the copy is, and
.B status
then fails on the parse error as before. Never re\-read by anything else.
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/wire\-drivers
Written by
.BR up :
the
.BR "ethtool \-i" " driver every present wire had at apply (empty when ethtool"
was not installed). The forwarding watchdog compares it when a wire's netdev
returns and reports an adapter that is not the one that was configured.
.B down
never reads it \(em NIC features are the host's own business now, not
cfab's.
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/conf\-sync\-attempted, RUN_DIR/conf\-sync\-committed
The daemon's generation state (decimal), under the declaration's run
directory.
.B attempted
is written before any apply, so a refused or reverted generation is never
retried, even across a daemon restart.
.TP
.I /etc/iproute2/rt_protos.d/cfab.conf
Names the kernel route\-protocol ids cfab and its engine install routes with, for
.B ip route
output only (
.BR cfab\-ospf " 201, " cfab\-static " 202, " cfab\-bgp " 203, "
.BR cfab\-other " 204 the engine's, " cfab\-return " 205 and " cfab\-default ,
206 cfab's own, outside the range the engine sweeps); cosmetic, cfab's own sweep matches the
numeric ids.
.TP
.I /etc/iproute2/rt_tables.d/cfab.conf
Names table 250
.B cfab\-default
for
.B ip route
and
.B ip rule
output the same way (see
.BR "HOST DEFAULT" );
cosmetic, cfab addresses the table by its number.
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/fabric.toml
The cluster-published declaration (pmxcfs; written by
.BR "conf publish" ).
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/gen
Publication generation counter (decimal; absent means 0).
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/acks/GEN/MEMBER
Per-member apply acknowledgments for generation
.IR GEN .
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/caps/MEMBER/cap\-DEV
Cluster-published measured capacities (written by
.BR measure\-cap ;
the shape derivation's fallback when the local cap file is absent, cached back
to it on first use).
.TP
.IR /etc/pve/priv/lock/cfab\-conf ", " /etc/pve/priv/lock/cfab\-measure
The publish cluster lock and the measurement lease (mkdir locks; pmxcfs
expires a crashed holder's lock after 120\ s).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/cfab.service
The packaged systemd unit (see
.BR SYSTEMD ).
.TP
.I /etc/default/cfab
Optional environment file for that unit (a conffile): where to set
.B CFAB_HOST
when this member's row is not named by the kernel hostname.
.SH SYSTEMD
The Debian package ships
.BR cfab.service ,
installed
.B disabled and not started
\(em installing cfab never changes the network. Write
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
first, then
.BR "systemctl enable \-\-now cfab" .
.PP
The unit is
.BR Type=notify .
.B ExecStart
is
.BR "cfab run" ,
the long-lived supervisor.
.B ExecReload
is
.BR "kill \-HUP $MAINPID" ,
which re\-reads the declaration (see
.B RELOAD
below).
.B ConditionPathExists=/etc/cfab/fabric.toml
skips it on a host that has the package but no declaration, so that host boots clean
instead of failing.
.B RestartPreventExitStatus=3\ 4
stops systemd from retrying a refused apply or a lost lock, while
.B SuccessExitStatus=6
and
.B RestartForceExitStatus=6
turn a changed\-declaration reload into a clean restart rather than a failure.
.PP
A package upgrade restarts the unit once the new files are in place (teardown and
re\-apply: every identity on the host is down for the restart). It is not left running
on the old binary: the supervisor respawns its children from
.IR /usr/bin/cfab ,
so an un\-restarted upgrade would run the next engine under the previous supervisor.
.PP
.B "apt remove"
stops the unit (correct: the binary is going away) and disables it;
.B "apt purge"
also removes
.IR /etc/default/cfab .
.SH METRICS
The supervisor serves this member's state as metrics on
.B 0.0.0.0:23232
at
.IR /metrics .
The body is OpenMetrics text, which a Prometheus\-compatible scraper also reads as
Prometheus text; the supervisor refreshes it from its own status gather every 15\ s, so a
scrape costs no host reads and reports the same numbers as
.BR "cfab status" .
The endpoint is plaintext and unauthenticated, like
.BR node_exporter (1):
put it behind a proxy or a firewall if LAN\-trust is not your posture. A supervisor that is not
running refuses the connection; before the first apply the scrape carries
.BR cfab_fabric_state{state="DOWN"} ,
the build and member info and the components, and no adjacency families; once applied it
carries the full schema. If the port cannot be bound, the supervisor logs one warning naming
the port and the errno, shows the standing
.B "cfab status"
line
.BR "metrics endpoint not listening on :23232 (...)" ,
and retries every 60\ s \(em the fabric is unaffected. A member with a
.B gw
zone also serves
.B cfab_host_default_path
(see
.B "HOST DEFAULT"
below): 0 while this member's own traffic takes cfab's additive default through the fabric
gateway (table 250), 1 while it takes the host's own floor default in main \(em because the
ingress prober reports the router unreachable, because the default was withdrawn, or because
cfab is not running; absent on a member with no
.B gw
zone and on one with no default route at all.
A member carrying at least one
.B [[workload]]
row also serves, all labeled
.BR {name} ,
and each absent (never a fabricated zero) for a row whose source field is
.B None
(deferred, or the read failed); a member with no row carries none of them:
.BR "cfab_workload_up" ,
1 when that row's table is present, its addresses and sibling return-path rule are installed,
the route-get proof succeeds, and its announcer is running;
.BR "cfab_workload_state{state}" ,
the row's classification, one of
.BR up / deferred / announcer_not_started / broken
with exactly one series 1;
.BR "cfab_workload_vms_seen" ,
VMs this member currently knows on the row's leg: neighbor entries inside its
.B prefix
in a resolved state (REACHABLE, STALE, DELAY, PROBE, PERMANENT), other than a declared
member address,
.BR gw
or
.BR router ,
whose MAC the uplink bridge holds on a non\-uplink port \(em the same derivation that
decides which /32s this member originates.
A VM on a peer host is learned through the uplink and counted there, not here;
a departed VM lingers as STALE until the kernel garbage\-collects the entry
(rack\-measured: minutes), so this counts neighbors known, not VMs alive \(em 0 is the
alert ("gateway up, nobody home"), a nonzero value is an upper bound;
.BR "cfab_workload_guard_drops_total{kind}" ,
the bridge guard's
.B claim
/
.B request
drop counters summed over the row's uplink ports, reset to 0 when
.B apply
re\-renders the table or the watchdog restores it;
.BR "cfab_workload_stray_forwards_total" ,
fabric packets this member refused to put on the row's leg because their destination is
not a VM it currently knows, summed over the row's one drop rule per allowed zone and
reset to 0 when
.B apply
re\-renders
.BR "inet cfab-fwd" ;
.BR "cfab_workload_rx_bytes_total" ,
.BR "cfab_workload_tx_bytes_total" ,
the gateway's own forwarding on the row's leg
(not cross\-host VM\-to\-VM traffic, which stays on the physical switch); and
.BR "cfab_workload_announces_total" ,
.BR "cfab_workload_bursts_total" ,
gratuitous ARPs and re\-announce bursts the row's announcer has sent (a rising burst rate is
flapping \(em MAC churn, watch deaths).
.SH WORKLOADS
A
.B [[workload]]
row declares a VM VLAN cfab reaches into the fabric:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[[workload]]
name   = "vms"
uplink = "primary"            # the host's vlan\-aware bridge carrying the VMs
vid    = 3                    # the VLAN the VMs are on; cfab's leg is cfab\-work\-vms
prefix = "192.168.20.0/24"
gw     = "192.168.20.254"     # the anycast gateway every host answers
router = "192.168.20.1"       # the VLAN's existing default router, printed inside DHCP option 121
allow  = ["storage"]          # zones this workload may reach; default deny, counted

[[member]]
name = "pve1"
workloads = [ { name = "vms", address = "192.168.20.2/24" } ]   # this host's own address on the leg
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B cfab check
refuses a
.BR gw ,
.B router
or member address outside
.BR prefix ;
any of them on the prefix's network or broadcast address; a
.B gw
or
.B router
that is not a bare IPv4 address (no mask \(em
.BR prefix 's
mask is applied to both); a member address
without the prefix's mask or equal to
.B gw
or
.BR router ;
.B gw
equal to
.BR router ;
two members declaring the same address on one workload row; an empty
.BR allow ;
an
.B allow
naming an unknown zone; a
.B vid
outside 2\-4094 (1 is the bridge's untagged default; 0 and 4095 are reserved);
a derived leg name colliding with a declared wire, a declared segment, or a
cfab\-generated bond/identity interface; two
.B [[workload]]
rows sharing a name, whose names cut to the same 15\-byte leg, or declaring the
same
.B uplink
and
.B vid
(one leg per bridge and vid);
a workload name that is also a zone name (workload and zone names share one
vocabulary); a workload
.B prefix
overlapping a zone's own
.B 10.<id>.0.0/16
block; a workload row no member carries; a member declaring the same
workload row twice; a member workload naming an unknown row;
.B "span = \(dqhost\(dq"
(phase 2, not built yet \(em omit
.B span
or write
.BR \(dqswitch\(dq );
an unrecognized
.B span
value; and a leaf
carrying one (a leaf never transits); with rows declared it also prints the
fabric aggregate (the smallest set of prefixes covering every declared zone
block) and the DHCP option 121 snippet. Names share the zone vocabulary, so
.B vms>storage
reads like
.BR storage>storage .
A fabric with
.B "[forward] enabled = false"
cannot declare a
.B [[workload]]
row at all: reaching a zone requires forwarding, so no
.B allow
value is ever valid once it is off.
.PP
.B cfab check
and
.B cfab up
refuse an
.B uplink
bridge that is not vlan\-aware
.RB ( "bridge\-vlan\-aware yes"
in
.IR /etc/network/interfaces ):
a tagged leg on it would receive nothing. Everything else is a wait, not a
refusal: a bridge that is not on the host yet, an uplink that cannot be
identified, an uplink that is not yet STP\-forwarding, and another vlan device
that already holds the row's
.B vid
on the
.B uplink
(a
.BI <bridge>. <vid>
host stanza, or any other tool that took that pair first \(em the kernel allows
one 802.1Q device per parent and vid, and cfab never claims the pair under that
name, so the leg cannot be built until the holder is removed) each defer that
one row to the watchdog (a warning names the reason and, for a holder, the
device to remove; no leg is built and no gw address goes live) and let the rest
of the member apply; the watchdog installs the row once the condition clears.
.PP
The bridge is the host's (Proxmox, systemd\-networkd, whatever brought it up)
and cfab never creates or deletes it. The leg on it is cfab's:
.B up
adds
.BR cfab\-work\-<name> ,
an 802.1Q vlan of
.B vid
on
.BR uplink ,
carrying this member's declared address, plus
.B vid
on the bridge itself (without it the leg receives nothing); IPv4 forwarding on
the leg;
a passive OSPF entry for the leg
in every allowed zone's instance; a pref\-2000 sibling return\-path rule per
(zone, workload prefix) on every member and leaf; a symmetric stateless
accept pair in the forward policy; a forward\-hook DSCP overwrite; the
anycast
.B gw
as a second address on the leg,
answered with the host's own MAC, with
.B net.ipv4.conf.all.arp_ignore=1
set; an nft bridge rule dropping ARP for
.B gw
on the bridge's uplink port; and a gratuitous\-ARP announcer that converges a
migrated VM's fabric\-side neighbor entries onto its new host.
.B down
removes everything
.B up
added on this member, the leg included, and \(em only if cfab is the one that
added it \(em the vid on the bridge; never the
.B uplink
bridge itself, and never a vid the host already had. The watchdog restores
anything of it found missing or wrong, the leg and the bridge's vid included.
.PP
A VM's DHCP lease should carry option 121 (RFC 3442) with the fabric
aggregate routed via
.BR gw ,
plus the default route via the declared
.B router
inside the
.I same
option: a client that receives option 121 ignores option 3, so a 121 lease
with no default route inside it leaves the VM with none at all. A VM without
option 121 still reaches fabric identities via the declared
.BR router ,
degraded, not broken. The aggregate is fabric\-wide: it covers every declared
zone block regardless of this row's own
.BR allow ,
so a zone the row does not reach still routes through
.B gw
rather than falling to the default route \(em traffic this workload is not
allowed into is dropped at the host, not carried off toward
.BR router .
.PP
.B "cfab status"
reports each row it carries as one line:
.IP
.nf
workload <name>: <uplink> vid <vid> (<leg>) <address> gw <gw> up|down, advertised to <zones>,
uplink <ports>, announce trigger <trigger>
.fi
.IP
followed by
.B ", N vms seen"
while the row is up and the neighbor read succeeded, and
.B ", N stray forwards"
while the forward chain carries the drop rule \(em one spelling for every
.IR N ,
including 0, never a fabricated zero otherwise (it is simply absent then). These are the same
counts
.B cfab_workload_vms_seen
and
.B cfab_workload_stray_forwards_total
above report.
.SH HOST DEFAULT
On a member with a
.B gw
zone, cfab additionally offers this host's own locally originated traffic the same path
outbound: an additive default route through the zone's router, installed in its own table so the
host's existing default is never touched.
.PP
The route lives in table
.B 250
(named
.B cfab\-default
by the package's
.I rt_tables.d
fragment):
.IP
.nf
default via <the gw zone's router> dev <the gw leg> src <this member's address on it>
table 250 proto cfab\-default
.fi
.IP
(kernel protocol id 206). With more than one
.B gw
zone, the first in
.I [[zone]]
order owns table 250. Three
.B "ip rule"
prefs surround it, installed as one unit \(em a failure anywhere takes back everything \(em and
kept level by the watchdog: pref
.B 2099
keeps the host's own admin addresses on
.BR main ,
one rule per address of the floor device
.RB ( "from <addr> iif lo lookup main" )
\(em keeps admin\-sourced replies (an off\-subnet ssh, a management UI) on the host's own path so
they never read as asymmetric at a zone firewall; and prefs
.B 2100
and
.B 2101
are the pair that reaches table 250: pref
.B 2100
.RB ( "from all iif lo lookup main suppress_prefixlength 0" )
makes the main table skip only its own default, so anything more specific still wins, and pref
.B 2101
.RB ( "from all iif lo lookup cfab\-default" )
then finds table 250.
.B "iif lo"
scopes both to traffic this host itself originates \(em a VM's or a leaf's transiting packet
never takes it.
.PP
The route is installed at apply optimistically \(em the prober has no opinion yet, and "no
opinion" must never read as "the router is dark" \(em then kept or withdrawn by the supervisor's
reconcile: it is withdrawn within about 2\ s of the router answering on no wire of the leg, and
reinstalled about 2\ s after it answers again (rack\-measured).
.B cfab_host_default_path
and
.B "cfab status"
read the same fact. A withdrawal takes only the route: the three prefs stay, and an empty table
250 falls through to the kernel's own
.B "32766 from all lookup main"
\(em an abruptly killed cfab is never a lockout. The floor default is never modified or removed
\(em "additive" means exactly
that: the ifupdown/DHCP default already in table
.B main
stays as it is, and with cfab gone (table 250 emptied, the three rules dropped) the kernel's own
final
.B "lookup main"
finds it exactly as it did before cfab ran. A pref\-2099 pin is dropped only once its address is
gone from every device on the host, not merely from the floor device for one tick (a bridge
reconfiguration can drop the floor default for a moment without moving the address) \(em an
early drop would send an established off\-subnet session's next packet over the fabric gateway
instead, asymmetric at a zone firewall. A host with no floor default of its own still gets the
fabric default (table 250, rules 2100/2101) but no pref\-2099 pins to protect it, and
.B "cfab up"
(and every re\-apply) prints one loud warning line saying so: a host is expected to keep its own
default route
(DHCP, or a static
.B gateway
line) rather than depend on cfab's, which exists to fail outward safely, not to be a host's only
path.
.PP
.B "cfab status"
names which path this member's own traffic takes right now:
.BR "default: <path>" ,
or
.B "default: <path>, <reason>"
once the path is not the fabric one.
.I path
is
.BR "via fabric gw <router> (<leg>)" ;
.BR "via floor <via> (<dev>)" ,
the host's own default in main; or
.BR none ,
when neither table holds one.
.I reason
is
.BR "router unreachable" ,
the prober says no wire of the leg reaches the gw zone's router;
.BR "no gw zone" ,
this member (every leaf included) never had a fabric default to offer; or
.BR withdrawn ,
table 250 is empty for any other reason \(em cfab is not running,
.B down
ran, or the install failed. For example:
.IP
.nf
default: via fabric gw 192.168.249.254 (cfab\-gw249)
default: via floor 192.168.1.1 (primary), router unreachable
.fi
